{"id":"h078","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Assume all gases are perfect unless stated otherwise. Unless otherwise stated, thermodynamic data are for 298.15 K. Calculate the final temperature of a sample of argon of mass $12.0 \\mathrm{~g}$ that is expanded reversibly and adiabatically from $1.0 \\mathrm{dm}^3$ at $273.15 \\mathrm{~K}$ to $3.0 \\mathrm{dm}^3$.\nIs the correct answer \"131 $\\mathrm{K}$\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"Argon is a monatomic perfect gas with C_V,m = 3/2 R.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The heat capacity ratio gamma for argon is 5/3.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"For a reversible adiabatic perfect-gas process, T V^(gamma-1) is constant.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The final temperature is T2 = T1 (V1/V2)^(2/3), which is about 131.3 K.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The mass and molar amount (about 0.30 mol) do not affect the final temperature.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Nothing in the problem requires a different heat capacity or exponent.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Because the process is reversible and adiabatic, the relation applies directly with no path correction.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The answer 131 K is correct.","confidence":0.96,"lean":1,"theta":1.508,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i"